          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric
            %strong theory of categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order
            axiomatisation of the concept of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            is (morally) an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic theory
            %em grosso modo
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+theory"} regular theory
            where existential quantification is provably unique.
          %p
            Historically, the first axiomatisation was given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} F. William Lawvere
            in his
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Functorial+Semantics+of+Algebraic+Theories"} dissertation
            in 1963 as part of a formalisation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+categories"} category of categories
            (cf. Lawvere (
            %a{:href => "#Law63},[1966](#Law66"}> 1963
            ) or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCC"} ETCC
            for further information).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature"} signature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_\mathbb{K}
            has two sorts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            for objects and arrows, respectively, three function symbols
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0:A\to O
            ,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_1:A\to O
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo :
                    %mi O
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id:O\to A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ↣
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
